WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:04.866
[SOUND].

00:00:04.866 --> 00:00:07.356
&gt;&gt; So, I'm thrilled to be here today.

00:00:07.356 --> 00:00:10.452
And when I was looking at my briefing
sheet and

00:00:10.452 --> 00:00:14.864
I saw that the title of the series is A
View from the Top.

00:00:14.864 --> 00:00:17.990
I thought, well, I guess I've been on the
top of an organization before, so

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:19.566
I can give some insights on that.

00:00:19.566 --> 00:00:23.187
But I thought what would be interesting is
that I've also sorta been trampled.

00:00:23.187 --> 00:00:28.575
And on the ground with people's feet on my
head, [NOISE] looking up.

00:00:28.575 --> 00:00:31.860
So I maybe I can give a little perspective
from that vantage point as well.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.490
You do not want to hear me give a big long
speech, and I am not gonna do that.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:41.540
I'm gonna reserved most of the time, for,
for a Q and A.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:46.560
But in thinking about leadership I, I was
sorta reflecting.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.330
And I thought okay, what, what would I
want to share with young people who are in

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:54.650
undergrad or graduate school today.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:58.455
And there are three things through my
experiences in DC and

00:00:58.455 --> 00:01:02.037
now with Students First that I think could
be helpful.

00:01:02.037 --> 00:01:04.513
Not in telling you specifically what you
should do or

00:01:04.513 --> 00:01:07.220
not do, but just to give you a sense of
perspective.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:10.530
So three things.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:11.440
Very simple.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:12.906
The first, this is,

00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:17.200
actually, this is a lesson that I learned,
when I was the chancellor.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:24.540
And it was one that came to me from a, a
mentor and hero of mine, Joel Klein.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:31.270
So, Joel is, used to be the the Chancellor
of the New York City school system.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:36.350
And long before I was working in DC, the
mayor of D.C.

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:40.410
and the council members were trying to
figure out.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:44.000
Whether they wanted to move to a mayoral
control model or not.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.460
So they took a trip to New York.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:48.760
They met with Joel Klein and Mike
Bloomberg.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.010
They toured the schools.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.090
They sorta saw what was going on there.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:57.263
And at the end of that visit Adrian Fenty
said to Joel Kline,

00:01:57.263 --> 00:02:02.453
okay I want somebody like you to come and
run the schools in D.C..

00:02:02.453 --> 00:02:05.990
Can you give me a recommendation?

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:09.540
And Joel actually ended up recommending me
for the job.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:13.050
And because of that he always felt this
incredible sense of guilt.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:18.090
And so he would call me and kinda you
know, check up on me all the time.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:22.626
So I remember one evening in the height of

00:02:22.626 --> 00:02:27.320
sorta my first 100 days when things were
really getting a little tough.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.080
I was driving home one night.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.175
It's raining outside.

00:02:32.175 --> 00:02:35.080
It's like 11 o'clock at night and my phone
rings.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.030
And I look at it and

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:39.990
I see that it's from Joel Klein, so I pull
over to the side of the road.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.360
Answer the phone and I said, yes sir.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.220
And he said how are you doing Michelle?

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:45.760
I said, I'm, I'm doing okay.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.370
It's a little tough right now, you know,
but I'm hanging in there.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:50.395
And he's like, he's like, that's good.

00:02:50.395 --> 00:02:55.190
He said, I, I'm calling to give you some
advice.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:57.100
And I said, okay, I am ready.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.880
And he said there are two things that I
wanna communicate to you tonight.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:04.010
I said okay, I'm, I'm ready, let's go.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:06.758
He said the first one, he said, do you
have a boyfriend?

00:03:06.758 --> 00:03:07.510
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.190
&gt;&gt; And I said no, cuz at the time I did
not.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:13.315
And he said okay, so my first piece of
advice is go out and get one.

00:03:13.315 --> 00:03:17.000
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I said, okay.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:22.350
I did not think that I would ever be
getting love life advice from Joel Klein.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:25.510
So I was sorta hesitatingly you know,
sorta said, oh, okay sir.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:28.650
And he said, here's what you need to
understand.

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:33.370
He said, this job is one of the loneliest
jobs you can possibly have.

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:36.600
He said, everyday is just an absolute
grind.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.680
He said, I would never be able to do what
I do everyday if I

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:41.730
didn't have my wife at home.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:45.510
Because at the end of every tough day I
get in to bed and

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:48.560
she goes, baby don't worry about it.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.770
She's like, you're not the crazy one.

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:52.030
They're the crazy ones.

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:52.725
You are okay.

00:03:52.725 --> 00:03:53.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.400
&gt;&gt; He said, if I didn't have that
grounding me everyday,

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.660
I would not be able to wake up every
morning and go to work.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:03.938
He said, so my first piece of advice is go
and find a boyfriend.

00:04:03.938 --> 00:04:04.900
I said, okay.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.610
I actually meanwhile did do that and
happily.

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:10.030
So I listened to that piece.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:13.040
And then, the second thing he said, he
sorta got really quiet and serious.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.230
And he said.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:15.740
So here's my second thing.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.500
He said, you have to lead from the front.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:25.900
And he said it with such conviction and
confidence that I said, okay, I got it.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:30.100
And I hung up the phone, and I pulled back
onto the road.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.210
And I'm thinking.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:33.850
I have no idea what that means, right?

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:34.470
But I didn't wanna,

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:37.700
I didn't wanna ask him any questions cuz I
didn't wanna seem like I was stupid.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.190
So, you know, I'd get back on the road.

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:42.985
And it actually wasn't until several
months later that I had

00:04:42.985 --> 00:04:47.050
sorta lived through a lot of the turmoil
of, of the, of being the chancellor.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:49.520
That I actually realized what he meant.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:50.140
Right?

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:53.880
Because when you are a leader, sometimes
you've gotta be out in front.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.050
You can see things that, that other people
can't see at the time.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:02.110
And if you get mired in the, the muck, if
you sorta get you know,

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:05.070
pulled into every single argument on one
side or another.

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:09.520
You're actually not gonna be able to move
your organization as far as you can,

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:10.860
as fast as you can.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.323
So when he gave me that piece of advice,

00:05:13.323 --> 00:05:17.068
I was actually in the middle of our school
closure process.

00:05:17.068 --> 00:05:18.855
Which was incredibly painful.

00:05:18.855 --> 00:05:21.665
You know, we had 144 schools, we were
closing 23 of them.

00:05:21.665 --> 00:05:23.245
It was 15% of the schools.

00:05:23.245 --> 00:05:27.033
And if you ever wanna quickly become the
most unpopular person in

00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:31.185
a city all you have to do is tell somebody
that you are closing a school.

00:05:31.185 --> 00:05:32.276
Much less 23 schools.

00:05:32.276 --> 00:05:33.873
So I was in the middle of this and

00:05:33.873 --> 00:05:37.840
it was, it was this tremendous amount of
opposition push back.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:42.540
And the interesting this was, about a year
after we closed the schools and

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:43.520
we went through that process.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.030
I was in one of the schools that had been
consolidated.

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:49.760
And I was trying to check out you know,
what the situation was.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:50.630
A women came up to me.

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.090
She said, hey, hey, hey look at me, you
recognize me.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:57.900
She said I was the one last year that was
always screaming at

00:05:57.900 --> 00:05:59.230
you about closing schools.

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:00.795
I said, oh yeah, I know who you are.

00:06:00.795 --> 00:06:03.890
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I could never forget that face.

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:05.060
She's like you know what?

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:06.710
I just wanted to let you know you were
right.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:09.690
She said I didn't, I didn't, we couldn't
see it at the time.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:12.920
Because it was such an emotional and
sentimental process for

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:16.240
us and we were just angry at you and you
know, this whole process.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.070
She's like, but now I get it.

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:19.500
Cuz we're in this school, and

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.790
this school has a lot more resources, our
kids are getting a better education.

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:25.166
So I'm good.

00:06:25.166 --> 00:06:27.255
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And she walks away.

00:06:27.255 --> 00:06:27.990
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:30.770
&gt;&gt; And I'm thinking, where's the
Washington Post when this kinda

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:31.884
stuff happens, right?

00:06:31.884 --> 00:06:35.664
But what I realize is this, we were, we
were able to,

00:06:35.664 --> 00:06:40.706
because we made the incredibly tough
decision to close those schools.

00:06:40.706 --> 00:06:44.120
We were able to create a new reality for
the children in Washington DC.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:48.980
Because of that decision, we were able to
provide an art music, an art teacher,

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:52.100
a music teacher, a PE teacher, a
librarian, a nurse.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:53.380
And a social worker or

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:57.010
a guidance counselor at every single
school in the city.

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:59.083
For the first time in the history,

00:06:59.083 --> 00:07:03.027
that anybody could ever remember, we
equalized the resources.

00:07:03.027 --> 00:07:05.849
So that having an art teacher wasn't
dependent on whether you,

00:07:05.849 --> 00:07:08.738
you know, were at one of the schools that
had wealthier parents.

00:07:08.738 --> 00:07:11.670
And they could hold an auction to hire the
teacher, etc.

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:13.330
We equalized the playing field for

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:18.770
all of the kids as it pertained to the
kinds of curriculum that they should have.

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:22.675
And had we got caught up in all of the
sorta emotion and, and

00:07:22.675 --> 00:07:25.082
the sorta opposition and whatnot.

00:07:25.082 --> 00:07:27.683
We would not have been able to follow
through on that decision.

00:07:27.683 --> 00:07:31.180
And we could not have provided the kids
with those additional resources.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:34.100
So that's my first message is sometimes,
you gotta lead from the front.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.365
My second, observation.

00:07:37.365 --> 00:07:42.140
[COUGH] And, and, you could, you could
either take my advice or not on this one.

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:46.790
But I believe very strongly that in order
to be an effective leader,

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:49.920
you have to be okay with not being liked.

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:52.895
And I'm like really really good at this
one.

00:07:52.895 --> 00:07:53.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:56.810
you can, you can, you can, you can take my
advice.

00:07:56.810 --> 00:08:04.737
I, I remember very clearly about halfway
through my second year as chancellor.

00:08:04.737 --> 00:08:08.640
A columnist for the Washington Post wrote
a piece.

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:12.100
And it was sorta you know, I like Michelle
Rhee.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:16.300
She is doing, you know, good things and
making hard decisions.

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:17.560
I just wish.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:18.630
She would be a little nicer.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.750
So I read the piece and I'm pissed off.

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:23.845
So I call the guy and I'm like what are
you doing?

00:08:23.845 --> 00:08:25.098
And he's like, no you don't understand.

00:08:25.098 --> 00:08:29.193
He's like I, I want you to be here for the
long haul, I think you are good for

00:08:29.193 --> 00:08:29.920
the system.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:34.380
He said, but in order for you to be here,
people got to like you a little bit more.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:39.051
You could be a little friendlier and
nicer.

00:08:39.051 --> 00:08:44.428
And I look and you know so I said to him I
said look you have to figure out what you

00:08:44.428 --> 00:08:50.050
think the most important characteristics
of a schools chancellor are.

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:55.280
And quite frankly if its sorta personality
driven then you know and

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:57.810
you want warm and fuzzy.

00:08:57.810 --> 00:08:59.910
Then I am not your girl.

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:02.138
And you should actually be advocating for
my ouster.

00:09:02.138 --> 00:09:07.538
But if you like what is going on in the
schools, if you believe sincerely

00:09:07.538 --> 00:09:12.490
that the right decisions are being made in
the interests of kids.

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:15.470
Then you should not be saying Michelle
really needs to change her personality.

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:19.329
You should be advocating that we kinda
leave the personalities aside and

00:09:19.329 --> 00:09:23.012
focus on the policies of what's actually
happening in the schools.

00:09:23.012 --> 00:09:25.237
So, I say this because you know,

00:09:25.237 --> 00:09:29.860
when I was in my first year as a
chancellor my mother came to town.

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:31.120
So, when your mom comes to town,

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:33.640
yo, you got a whole different perspective
on things.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:39.570
So, my mom happened to come at a time
where people were very, very angry at me.

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:43.330
And so she comes into town, it was all on
the school closure stuff.

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:45.490
And she, you know, opens up the Washington
Post.

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:49.290
There's a two-page spread on, you know,
all the different schools that I'm

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.630
closing, like, can point it all over the
city.

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:54.990
She turns on the TV, there are people,
like, you know,

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:58.674
throwing things at me at a meeting, and
that sorta thing.

00:09:58.674 --> 00:10:02.300
She [LAUGH] she, over the weekend said,
they're.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:05.120
Yeah, there's some really loud

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:08.680
people down the block who are picketing
about something.

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:10.910
And I said, yeah, they're picketing me.

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:11.850
She's like, oh my gosh.

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:13.510
I can't believe this is going on.

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:16.460
So anyways, one night at the end of

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:19.590
a long night of community meetings that I
was having.

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:21.410
I come home, I'm in the kitchen,

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:23.120
I'm making myself a peanut better an jelly
sandwich.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:28.590
My mother sorta creeps into the kitchen,
and she says are you okay?

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:29.900
And I say yeah I'm fine I'm good.

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:31.350
And she looks at me, she goes you know,

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:37.580
when you were little you never used to
care what people thought about you.

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:38.290
She's like, and so

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:41.720
I always thought that you were going to
grow up to be really antisocial.

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:43.685
But I see now that this is serving you
well.

00:10:43.685 --> 00:10:48.380
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I said yes, it was.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:53.740
Because the bottom line is that if you are
concerned in too

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:55.720
strong a way with being liked.

00:10:55.720 --> 00:11:00.960
With whether or not you're popular, with
what your polling numbers look like, etc.

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:04.240
Then you're gonna make a different set of
decisions.

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:09.960
And the bottom line is that no really
significant change comes

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:13.300
without some pretty significant pushback
and opposition as well.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:14.770
And at the end of the day,

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:17.740
if the decisions that you're making are
the ones that you believe are right.

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:22.340
And you, you, you, you, you, you have the
confidence of that.

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:24.490
Then you have to be okay getting that
opposition,

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:28.300
getting the criticism and, at the end of
the day, not being liked so much.

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:31.480
So that's my second piece of advice.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:38.440
My third is actually about my boss at the
time when I was in D.C., Adrian Fenty.

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:42.520
Who was the mayor of D.C. and he's the
person that I reported to.

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:48.090
And in my opinion, he is the person
without whom none

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:51.090
of what we did in DC could have happened.

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:54.640
He was an extraordinarily unique
politician.

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:58.800
In that when he hired me he said, all I
want you to do is make the schools better.

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:05.950
Make the schools a place where we wanna
send our own children every day.

00:12:05.950 --> 00:12:09.090
In terms of politics and the pushback and
what, I'll handle that.

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:11.750
But we're not gonna worry about it.

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:15.050
Because at the end of the day, we're gonna
make every decision within what we

00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:17.480
believe is in the best interests of kids.

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:19.560
He said that on Day 1 to me.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:22.460
And of all the crazy things that happened
to me in DC,

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:24.830
actually, none of that was particularly
surprising.

00:12:24.830 --> 00:12:27.550
The one thing that I would say was
surprising to me is the fact that

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:30.630
that man never wavered one time.

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:32.550
In fact, this is a quick story.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:41.202
His primary election, was in September of
2010.

00:12:41.202 --> 00:12:47.660
And in August, we had just implemented a
new teacher evaluation system.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:51.412
Where for the first time, we were
evaluating teachers based in part on

00:12:51.412 --> 00:12:55.624
whether or not their student achievement
levels had grown in the school.

00:12:55.624 --> 00:12:59.466
And so we had identified a couple hundred
people who were,

00:12:59.466 --> 00:13:01.876
who were categorized as ineffective.

00:13:01.876 --> 00:13:08.090
And based on the, the framework and the
structure that we had set out.

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:11.404
We said that those people who had been
rated as ineffective after their first

00:13:11.404 --> 00:13:12.970
year were subject to termination.

00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:16.720
But this is weeks away from his primary
election.

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:18.610
So I call the man and I say, okay mayor,
so

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:21.856
here's where we are and reminded him about
the new evaluation system.

00:13:21.856 --> 00:13:24.710
I said, you know the decision is whether
we

00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:28.370
actually move forward with this
terminations or not.

00:13:28.370 --> 00:13:32.550
Because at the end of the day I am very
sensitive to the fact that

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:34.550
you've got a race coming up.

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:37.270
I don't want to do anything to jeopardize
your ability to,

00:13:37.270 --> 00:13:40.950
to you know, run the city effectively,
etc.

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:42.490
And he said let me ask you a question.

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:47.830
He said, if we take this action, will that
ensure that

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:52.360
kids in D.C. have better teachers in, come
fall?

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:52.988
And I said yes.

00:13:52.988 --> 00:13:53.890
He said then we gotta do it.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:55.700
I said, really?

00:13:55.700 --> 00:13:56.360
He said, absolutely.

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:59.025
Because every other political calculus
that we make [NOISE] we

00:13:59.025 --> 00:14:00.866
don't know what the outcome is gonna be.

00:14:00.866 --> 00:14:01.600
Right?
So

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:05.330
if we don't make this decision does that
mean I'm gonna win the election?

00:14:05.330 --> 00:14:05.960
Can't, we can't tell.

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:08.660
And that's not really at the end of the
day what matters.

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:09.340
It's not why we're here.

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:11.810
We're here to make sure that the kids are
getting a great education.

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:15.390
And if you can tell me that by removing
these people and bringing in other ones,

00:14:15.390 --> 00:14:19.310
that kids are gonna get a, a good
education, then I'm okay with that.

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:22.230
So it was exactly the answer that I wanted
to hear.

00:14:22.230 --> 00:14:24.370
It was not so much the answer that other
people wanted to hear.

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:28.810
So we made the decision and you know the
city goes nuts, right?

00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:31.840
And it, I started getting calls from
people, all throughout the city.

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:35.590
People who were supporters of the Mayor
and mine saying, are you insane?

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:37.690
This is political suicide.

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:42.180
You can't fire a bunch of people weeks
before the election.

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:45.450
And it was amazing to me how despite the
fact that he was

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:50.620
being told that his political career was
potentially gonna end with this decision.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:55.830
The ease and the confidence with which he
made it, because he knew that, at,

00:14:55.830 --> 00:15:00.094
at the end of the day, it was the right
policy decision to make.

00:15:00.094 --> 00:15:05.158
I wish I could say the same for
politicians across this county, and

00:15:05.158 --> 00:15:08.330
elected officials across this country.

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:12.750
We live in a state where there is,

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:16.210
to say a lack of political courage is an
understatement.

00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:19.848
We live in a state where not too many
months ago,

00:15:19.848 --> 00:15:24.232
when it was found that in an L.A. USD in
Los Angeles, there was

00:15:24.232 --> 00:15:30.214
a teacher who had been a sexual predator,
kind of molesting kids and et cetera.

00:15:30.214 --> 00:15:36.242
A state assemblyman from L.A. introduced a
piece of legislation and

00:15:36.242 --> 00:15:39.817
all it would have done is made it easier
for

00:15:39.817 --> 00:15:44.736
school districts to fire people were
sexual predators.

00:15:44.736 --> 00:15:47.744
That's a pretty low bar in my opinion,

00:15:47.744 --> 00:15:51.492
that bill didn't even make it out of
committee.

00:15:51.492 --> 00:15:56.117
Couldn't even go to the floor of the
legislature for a vote, because the status

00:15:56.117 --> 00:16:00.671
quo people, and, you know, folks who
wanted to defend, you know, the adult

00:16:00.671 --> 00:16:05.940
interests etc said to the politicians,
don't vote for this, etc, etc.

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:09.359
Couldn't even get a bill like that out of
committee.

00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:15.030
Couple of weeks ago, we had a similar
situation where

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:20.780
a legislator introduced a bill around
teacher evaluation in the state.

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:23.470
It was a very very low key bill.

00:16:23.470 --> 00:16:25.380
It just said three things.

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:29.890
One that parents and the community should
have input into the evaluation.

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:34.820
Two, that we should evaluate teachers and
put them into four

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:39.610
different performance bands as opposed to
just satisfactory or unsatisfactory.

00:16:39.610 --> 00:16:44.320
And that for veteran teachers, we would
move the timeframe.

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:48.920
So instead of a evaluating them once every
five years,

00:16:48.920 --> 00:16:50.630
we could evaluate them once every three
years.

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:58.220
The committee vote of nine committee
members, six of them abstained.

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:02.440
Abstained.

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:04.990
Which is, to me, I was like, look, if you
don't like the bill for

00:17:04.990 --> 00:17:06.830
whatever reason, then vote against it.

00:17:06.830 --> 00:17:09.050
But what are, what's abstaining all about?

00:17:09.050 --> 00:17:14.150
So, our, our, we had a bunch of students
first members at the, at the capitol.

00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:16.215
And they were asking one of the people who
had,

00:17:16.215 --> 00:17:19.410
and saying you know, why don't you take a
stance on this?

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:21.130
I mean, what do you believe about it?

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:22.850
Whether it's right or wrong, you'll agree
[UNKNOWN].

00:17:22.850 --> 00:17:24.950
But you, you have to take a stand.

00:17:24.950 --> 00:17:29.400
And the guy said well, here's the thing
I've heard from people,

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:33.080
constituents in my community on both
sides.

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:35.160
And so, no matter what happens,

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:38.750
there's gonna be one group of people
that's unhappy.

00:17:38.750 --> 00:17:40.970
And so I don't want to be in the middle of
that.

00:17:40.970 --> 00:17:46.000
But that's your job, right?

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:49.130
Your job is to hear from both sides.

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:50.970
What the pros and cons of something like
this.

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:53.840
And then to make a decision that you
believe is in the public interest.

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:57.320
Right, that is gonna serve your
constituents best.

00:17:57.320 --> 00:18:00.570
Whether or not people get angry at you,
that can't play into the calculus.

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:05.520
Hardly the profiles of courage that you
would like to

00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:07.810
see from your elected officials.

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:10.752
So at the end of the day my, my third
point, and

00:18:10.752 --> 00:18:14.724
potential one of my most important is you
have to as a leader you have to

00:18:14.724 --> 00:18:17.700
have the courage of your convictions.

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:21.640
You have to know what it is you believe is
right and

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:25.165
then you have to be willing to stake
everything, essentially, on it.

00:18:25.165 --> 00:18:26.850
Whether it's your job or your staying in
office.

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:30.760
So many of these politicians like, well,
here's the thing, if I take this vote,

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:32.670
then these people are not gonna like me.

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:33.830
These people are not gonna vote for

00:18:33.830 --> 00:18:36.810
me next time and the world is a better
place if I remain in office.

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:41.400
And what I would argue is the world is
only a better place if you're in office.

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:43.730
If you're actually making the decisions
that you think are right for

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:44.860
your constituents.

00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:48.040
If not and you're just, you know, trying
to determine which way the political winds

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:51.920
are blowing that's actually not moving us
forward in the right direction.

00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:55.350
So having the courage of your convictions
is my third.

00:18:55.350 --> 00:18:58.180
But at the end of the day here's what I
believe in.

00:18:58.180 --> 00:19:01.940
Take questions and you can ask me anything
you want.

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:06.670
Here's why I feel these three lessons, as
it

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:11.950
pertains to this incredibly important
field of education, is so important

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:17.190
because public education is supposed to be
the great equalizer in this country.

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:20.190
It is supposed to be the thing that
ensures it doesn't matter

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:22.130
if you're black or white, rich or poor.

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:24.980
We have a public education system so

00:19:24.980 --> 00:19:29.260
that every single kid can have an equal
shot in life, right?

00:19:29.260 --> 00:19:33.010
You work hard you do the right thing you
can live the American dream.

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:37.500
Unfortunately, that is not the reality for
the,

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:41.390
for a huge percentage of kids in our
country today.

00:19:41.390 --> 00:19:43.960
In fact, In America today,

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:49.900
America, is towards the bottom
internationally on social mobility.

00:19:49.900 --> 00:19:53.800
Which means that if you are a child who is
born into poverty in this country,

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:56.790
the likelihood that you will ever escape
poverty is slim to none.

00:19:57.810 --> 00:19:58.510
Why?

00:19:58.510 --> 00:20:02.700
Because if you live in a poor community,
with not a lot of resources,

00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:06.040
the likelihood is very high that you
attend a failing school.

00:20:06.040 --> 00:20:09.780
And that you will not gain the skills and
knowledge that you need to be able to go

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:12.790
onto higher education, get a high paying
job, et cetera.

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:18.290
So we are essentially saying to poor kids
now, you don't have a shot in life.

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:19.830
Because of the circumstance of your birth.

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:25.190
That in my opinion is the most terrifying
dynamic that

00:20:25.190 --> 00:20:27.310
we have going on in this country today.

00:20:27.310 --> 00:20:33.570
It goes against every single ideal that we
stand for as a nation.

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:36.030
We're supposed to be the land of equal
opportunity,

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:38.920
where anybody can pull themselves up by
their bootstraps.

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:42.850
If you have the, the, the wherewithal the,
the, the,

00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:45.520
the will, right, you can do this.

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:50.430
But if we are ensuring that essentially we
are keeping poor kids trapped in

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:53.940
failing schools, where they're not getting
the education they deserve.

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:55.060
And then they can't live that life.

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:57.720
What does that say about us as a nation?

00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:02.570
I would argue that it is everything that
we don't wanna say.

00:21:02.570 --> 00:21:06.010
It goes, it, again it goes against
everything we stand for, and

00:21:06.010 --> 00:21:11.590
that is why if we are to focus on one
issue in a,

00:21:11.590 --> 00:21:18.390
in a collective, and bipartisan way in
this country, it has to be this topic.

00:21:18.390 --> 00:21:21.850
Every single person in this room plays a
role in this.

00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:23.268
Unless you stand up for

00:21:23.268 --> 00:21:28.281
kids, then we can't expect that anything,
anything is ever going to change.

00:21:28.281 --> 00:21:31.950
Okay, that is the end of my remarks, and I
then I will take questions.

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:41.609
[APPLAUSE].

00:21:41.609 --> 00:21:45.324
&gt;&gt; Okay, I think we have two people with
mics, so I will just go back and forth.

00:21:45.324 --> 00:21:47.065
Over there's the first.

00:21:47.065 --> 00:21:47.785
&gt;&gt; So thank you for coming.

00:21:47.785 --> 00:21:48.366
My name's Jamal.

00:21:48.366 --> 00:21:49.457
I'm a second year MBA student.

00:21:49.457 --> 00:21:51.361
I wanna challenge you a little bit on the,

00:21:51.361 --> 00:21:54.405
the notion, the idea of being friendly
versus being tough.

00:21:54.405 --> 00:21:58.228
We spend a lot of time here studying the
interpersonal intricacies and

00:21:58.228 --> 00:22:02.697
making tough decisions and something that
I've come to learn is that there is a lot

00:22:02.697 --> 00:22:07.165
of middle ground sometimes and I wonder,
when you reflect upon your time in D.C,

00:22:07.165 --> 00:22:11.376
do you ever think that had you moderated
your personality, your toughness,

00:22:11.376 --> 00:22:13.310
you might of been more effective?

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:16.190
I mean, for example, you, you've fired
friends while on TV.

00:22:16.190 --> 00:22:18.640
And I know you've, you said you regret
that in hindsight, but

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:20.310
if that's indicative.

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:22.610
Of kind of your general disposition,

00:22:22.610 --> 00:22:26.100
do you feel like you ever did a disservice
to students by being tough to a fault?

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:33.020
&gt;&gt; So, here's what, what I said is you,
you, you, you have to be okay,

00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:37.531
with not being liked, I didn't say you
gotta be an a-hole, right?

00:22:38.810 --> 00:22:41.120
And here's, and there's a difference
between those two things.

00:22:41.120 --> 00:22:44.620
So I would go out all the time, out into
the community.

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:48.360
and, and, you know, one of the things that
I would often do, sort of,

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:52.510
is speak to large crowds of folks, or I
would do living room meetings where,

00:22:52.510 --> 00:22:55.840
you know, a constituent could invite me in
to meet with, you know, 12 or

00:22:55.840 --> 00:22:56.880
15 of their neighbours.

00:22:56.880 --> 00:22:59.570
And oftentimes after those kinds of
meetings, people would say,

00:22:59.570 --> 00:23:02.770
wow you're nothing like we thought you
were gonna be like.

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:08.020
You're actually kinda funny and charming,
look, so I can win people over.

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:10.710
And I, you know, I tried to do plenty of
that.

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:14.060
But at the end of the day the question is,
what is more important,

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:15.290
what was more important to me.

00:23:15.290 --> 00:23:19.540
Being liked and being popular or doing the
right thing for kids?

00:23:19.540 --> 00:23:22.550
And, I remember, I don't know if any of
you saw the movie Waiting for Superman.

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:27.240
But there's a scene in that movie where
I'm in a,

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:30.280
I'm in a community meeting where people
are, like, going nuts.

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:33.530
And apparently I have this look on my
face, this sort of serene look.

00:23:33.530 --> 00:23:37.530
And so people ask me all the time, like,
what were you thinking?

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:39.780
Because, you know, people are yelling at
you and

00:23:39.780 --> 00:23:42.190
calling you every name in the book and,
like, you look so calm.

00:23:42.190 --> 00:23:46.270
So I was trying to literally think back
to, like, what was my mindset?

00:23:46.270 --> 00:23:47.320
Right?

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:50.790
And, at the end of the day, I actually
know what was going through my head.

00:23:50.790 --> 00:23:54.010
Which was, we are living in a city

00:23:55.340 --> 00:24:01.054
where 8% of the eighth graders are
operating on grade level in mathematics.

00:24:01.054 --> 00:24:01.774
8%.

00:24:01.774 --> 00:24:05.320
92% of our kids do not have the skills and

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:08.340
knowledge necessary to be productive
members of society.

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:11.710
And, we have to do something radical to
change that.

00:24:11.710 --> 00:24:15.390
You may not like it, you can scream at me
all you want.

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:19.520
But I am not gonna let this continue on my
watch.

00:24:20.600 --> 00:24:25.900
So I'm good with, but, you know, be, if
you can go out there and, and,

00:24:25.900 --> 00:24:28.590
and win people over, absolutely you should
try.

00:24:28.590 --> 00:24:30.700
But if that becomes your goal.

00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:33.050
Right?
That becomes the outcome that

00:24:33.050 --> 00:24:33.970
you're seeking.

00:24:33.970 --> 00:24:35.090
That's what I think is problematic.

00:24:38.140 --> 00:24:39.510
&gt;&gt; So.
&gt;&gt; Yes?

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:41.550
&gt;&gt; Thanks for coming to Stanford.

00:24:41.550 --> 00:24:46.410
The question I ask is, how do you take
your values and

00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:48.370
translate them into school financing?

00:24:48.370 --> 00:24:53.770
And I'm thinking about, you're in
Sacramento the state

00:24:53.770 --> 00:24:59.490
treasurer invests a ton of money through
banks, is there an opportunity for

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:04.075
social impact bonds or some other
financing mechanism that would have

00:25:04.075 --> 00:25:09.362
a groomer rationality let's say then the
budgeting process in, in the capital.

00:25:09.362 --> 00:25:13.230
&gt;&gt; Cuz let's be clear the current
budgeting process

00:25:13.230 --> 00:25:15.220
is the least rational process around.

00:25:16.940 --> 00:25:21.030
So here's, here's, here's the, the bottom
line.

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:25.910
Should we bring some, some reason and
ration to how we spend money in schools?

00:25:25.910 --> 00:25:26.520
Absolutely 100%.

00:25:26.520 --> 00:25:32.320
It is much more difficult than you think
it would be for a number of reasons.

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:37.290
First, is, what is widely sort of known
and touted and

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:38.860
talked about in this country.

00:25:38.860 --> 00:25:42.870
And in fact if you go to any school
district board meeting around this time,

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:43.700
which is budgeting time.

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:47.820
What you're gonna hear anywhere across the
country is what we need in order to

00:25:47.820 --> 00:25:49.460
fix the system is more money.

00:25:49.460 --> 00:25:51.500
But then actually the data doesn't bear
that out.

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:53.420
Right?
The data shows that over the last two to

00:25:53.420 --> 00:25:56.970
three decades in this country, we have
more than doubled, and

00:25:56.970 --> 00:26:00.660
almost tripled the amount of money that we
are spending per kid in public education.

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:02.860
That's controlling for inflation.

00:26:02.860 --> 00:26:04.660
And yet the results have remained pretty
much stagnant.

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:09.940
We, we are spending more money per kid
than

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:13.620
almost any other OECD nation out there.

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:16.330
And yet, again, the results are not where
we would want them to be.

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:22.960
I can tell you, from having led a school
system where we were spending

00:26:22.960 --> 00:26:27.130
more money per kid than any, almost any
other jurisdiction in the entire nation,

00:26:27.130 --> 00:26:30.810
that people still were saying, we need
more money, we need more money.

00:26:30.810 --> 00:26:32.920
And when I looked at the budget we were
spending,

00:26:32.920 --> 00:26:36.430
we had a one billion dollar budget when I
got to DC, and

00:26:36.430 --> 00:26:40.040
of that 403 million of it was going into
the classroom and the schools.

00:26:40.040 --> 00:26:45.010
That means the majority was being sucked
out by this

00:26:45.010 --> 00:26:51.760
bureaucratic bloated bureaucracy we had no
idea there's no accountability, etc.

00:26:51.760 --> 00:26:54.110
And so when you talk to people in
California today,

00:26:54.110 --> 00:26:55.210
you'll hear some similar things.

00:26:55.210 --> 00:26:59.270
Like if you go out, any, to any score any
superintendent, and

00:26:59.270 --> 00:27:02.780
ask them, how much money do we spend in
California per kid.

00:27:02.780 --> 00:27:05.250
You will get an answer of somewhere
between 5500 and $6500.

00:27:05.250 --> 00:27:08.250
That's what people will tell you.

00:27:08.250 --> 00:27:10.080
Actually that is not correct.

00:27:10.080 --> 00:27:15.160
In this state we are spending about $9500,
per kid per year on education.

00:27:16.330 --> 00:27:19.820
Those people actually are not wrong,
because there is a whole

00:27:19.820 --> 00:27:25.110
lot of money that they never see in the
schools cuz it gets lopped off the top and

00:27:25.110 --> 00:27:28.140
put towards our unfunded pension
liabilities et cetera.

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:31.630
In fact, unfunded pension liabilities are
a huge problem across the country.

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:37.490
There's just a, a piece of research that
showed that just funding

00:27:37.490 --> 00:27:41.470
unfunded pension liabilities in a place
like Pennsylv, in Philadelphia right now,

00:27:41.470 --> 00:27:46.430
is costing that district between 2 and
$3,000 per kid per year.

00:27:46.430 --> 00:27:48.380
We gotta fix that problem.

00:27:48.380 --> 00:27:51.160
Because until we fix that we can't say
there's not enough mon,

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:52.610
it's just where the money is going.

00:27:53.930 --> 00:27:57.300
So what we believe in is advocating for

00:27:57.300 --> 00:28:00.740
transparency as it pertains to the, to the
dollars.

00:28:00.740 --> 00:28:03.170
Now this is an interesting one cuz I was
just in Ohio the other day and

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:05.460
we were advocating for transparency of
dollars.

00:28:05.460 --> 00:28:09.510
And what we found was that there was a
school district in the state of Ohio,

00:28:09.510 --> 00:28:14.200
that was spending more money per kid on
their cheerleading program than on

00:28:14.200 --> 00:28:15.880
their literacy programs.

00:28:15.880 --> 00:28:16.380
I kid you not.

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:20.240
And I thought, this is actually a good
data point.

00:28:20.240 --> 00:28:23.370
Because I can't tell you that in that
community those people wouldn't say,

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:25.600
yes, cheerleading is so important.

00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:28.890
I grew up in Ohio, so I know, people like
their cheerleading.

00:28:28.890 --> 00:28:32.020
The community might have said, yes, we, we
value cheerleading more highly than

00:28:32.020 --> 00:28:35.530
literacy, in which case, then, okay, so be
it.

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:38.530
But I would be that there would be a whole
lot of people who'd say,

00:28:38.530 --> 00:28:41.450
wait a second, this is crazy and we ought
to change that.

00:28:41.450 --> 00:28:45.230
But until you have the data to show where
the dollars are going and

00:28:45.230 --> 00:28:48.330
what kind of return on, on investment
you're getting for those dollars,

00:28:48.330 --> 00:28:52.570
they're, it's gonna be very difficult to
advocate for the changes that we need.

00:28:52.570 --> 00:28:55.910
And it's, and I think it's always gonna
devolve down into let's just you know,

00:28:55.910 --> 00:28:57.120
let's just ask for more money.

00:28:58.730 --> 00:28:59.640
I'm not against more money.

00:28:59.640 --> 00:29:00.510
Let me be clear.

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:02.290
I'm just saying [LAUGH] that you,

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:05.470
you have to use the dollars that you have
right now well,

00:29:05.470 --> 00:29:09.750
before in, increased dollars we're gonna
have a significant return on investment.

00:29:11.730 --> 00:29:15.950
&gt;&gt; Hi thanks so much for being here with
us [COUGH] I had a question on there there

00:29:15.950 --> 00:29:21.530
are many areas on ed reform many different
angles that you could you could

00:29:21.530 --> 00:29:25.870
attack the issue with and I'm wondering
where where are you seeing the most.

00:29:25.870 --> 00:29:27.490
Like where, where are you most excited
about it?

00:29:27.490 --> 00:29:31.470
Is it like federal, federal level, state
level, district level, or is it

00:29:31.470 --> 00:29:36.750
charters or other kind of groups like your
own that you're currently leading, thanks?

00:29:36.750 --> 00:29:39.780
&gt;&gt; So I would say this.

00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:43.920
One of the, the challenges that we face in
education reform,

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:48.070
is that everybody is looking for the
silver bullet solution.

00:29:48.070 --> 00:29:48.991
Right.
So, I,

00:29:48.991 --> 00:29:54.216
on a monthly basis, I'll get calls from
governors across the country saying okay,

00:29:54.216 --> 00:29:56.880
Michelle, I'm gonna take education on.

00:29:56.880 --> 00:29:58.888
Tell me the one thing that I should do.

00:29:58.888 --> 00:30:02.220
&gt;&gt; And usually, I refuse to answer that
question.

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:05.471
Because I say, there is no one thing that
you can do that is going to

00:30:05.471 --> 00:30:07.810
change education in your state.

00:30:07.810 --> 00:30:12.940
It is much too simplistic a, a mindset to
go in to this with.

00:30:12.940 --> 00:30:16.490
So, take Students First, for example, my,
my new organization.

00:30:16.490 --> 00:30:19.890
We focus on three areas.

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:21.710
We don't think, think they're the only
three areas.

00:30:21.710 --> 00:30:23.440
They're just the ones that we focus on.

00:30:23.440 --> 00:30:26.100
It's principal and teacher equality.

00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:30.160
We focus on empowering parents with
information and choices.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:34.500
And we focus on fiscal accountability and
responsibility.

00:30:34.500 --> 00:30:40.500
Within those three areas, there are 37
different policies on our agenda.

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:45.300
And our belief is that you have to have
all 37 in place, in order to create

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:49.410
the kind of environment that's gonna be
ripe for real reform to take place.

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:51.220
But what people wanna do is just choose
one.

00:30:51.220 --> 00:30:54.720
So somebody will say, well I'm gonna pass
the parent trigger.

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:56.260
Or, I believe in vouchers.

00:30:56.260 --> 00:30:59.190
And then they believe that, you know, you
implement a voucher program and

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:02.080
then all of a sudden, everything's gonna
change cuz the market is gonna rule.

00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:05.330
It just doesn't work that way.

00:31:05.330 --> 00:31:09.430
So, part of, I think, we have to focus on
is understanding that,

00:31:10.510 --> 00:31:14.665
how the, the, the system became the way
that it is was very sort of,

00:31:14.665 --> 00:31:18.240
a complex mix of issues all sort of coming
together.

00:31:18.240 --> 00:31:22.670
And how we're gonna solve this problem is
also a complex mix of lots of

00:31:22.670 --> 00:31:24.040
different things happening.

00:31:24.040 --> 00:31:27.110
It's not just about one issue or one
policy.

00:31:27.110 --> 00:31:27.610
Yes.

00:31:31.170 --> 00:31:31.950
&gt;&gt; Hi.
I'm Jenny.

00:31:31.950 --> 00:31:33.740
Thanks for being here.

00:31:33.740 --> 00:31:36.190
I appreciate your talking about leading
from the front and

00:31:36.190 --> 00:31:39.930
be willing to take risks to not always be
popular.

00:31:39.930 --> 00:31:42.910
Another part of leadership that I am
trying to think about is how do you make

00:31:42.910 --> 00:31:46.570
sure that you're as inclusive as you can
be and really build a cohesive movement.

00:31:46.570 --> 00:31:49.670
So I'm wondering if you could talk about
how you think about balancing those two

00:31:49.670 --> 00:31:50.770
thing, things.

00:31:50.770 --> 00:31:53.460
And whether there's maybe been a time in
your leadership where you've

00:31:53.460 --> 00:31:54.810
decided to slow down or

00:31:54.810 --> 00:31:57.700
back off from one of your goals in order
to bring more people along with you.

00:31:57.700 --> 00:31:58.800
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:31:58.800 --> 00:32:00.120
That's a great question.

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:02.340
And, and something that to tell you the
truth.

00:32:02.340 --> 00:32:07.268
We weren't able to do particularly
effectively in D.C. so,

00:32:07.268 --> 00:32:09.810
but this is the lesson that I learned from
that.

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:10.780
What, when I was in D.C.

00:32:10.780 --> 00:32:13.730
we were implementing all of these
incredibly aggressive reforms.

00:32:13.730 --> 00:32:16.450
We were out in the community a ton.

00:32:16.450 --> 00:32:20.590
I mean, I, every single night we held
chancellors policy forums.

00:32:20.590 --> 00:32:23.230
I had office hours, I was doing living
room visits.

00:32:23.230 --> 00:32:26.300
I did you know, teacher talks twice a
week.

00:32:26.300 --> 00:32:30.850
I mean, we were out there trying to engage
the community in what, in,

00:32:30.850 --> 00:32:33.650
in our reform efforts getting their
feedback, etc, etc.

00:32:33.650 --> 00:32:37.200
At the end of the day, it didn't work.

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:42.420
We were not able to create an environment
where people felt that they were

00:32:42.420 --> 00:32:45.130
part of what we were creating.

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:47.040
And so, it's interesting because people
say to me,

00:32:47.040 --> 00:32:49.780
well this is, that, that shows that you,
know, you can't bring people along.

00:32:49.780 --> 00:32:51.340
I'm like, no it doesn't.

00:32:51.340 --> 00:32:53.830
What it shows is that I wasn't successful
at it.

00:32:53.830 --> 00:32:57.190
But just because I couldn't do something,
I would never say that therefore,

00:32:57.190 --> 00:32:58.830
it can't be done, right?

00:32:58.830 --> 00:33:03.050
So what I would hope is that the next
superintendent who comes along,

00:33:03.050 --> 00:33:07.430
whether it's in D.C. or somewhere else,
would be able to look at what we did.

00:33:07.430 --> 00:33:10.980
The things that we did well and the things
that we didn't do particularly well.

00:33:10.980 --> 00:33:13.960
And then, learn from those things and

00:33:13.960 --> 00:33:17.000
build off of them to try to chart a new
course.

00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:19.570
Like that's what I think the most
important thing was.

00:33:19.570 --> 00:33:23.600
So striking that balance I do think is
important and it was unfortunately

00:33:23.600 --> 00:33:28.290
something that we were never able to like,
do exactly the way that we wanted to do.

00:33:28.290 --> 00:33:31.960
I think that had we been able to stay for
another term for

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:36.130
four more years I do believe strongly that
that dynamic would have changed.

00:33:36.130 --> 00:33:38.800
Because for us, because what we were doing
was so

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:42.000
dramatic and we were doing it in such
short order.

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:47.110
We were getting that kind of immediate you
know, opposition and,

00:33:47.110 --> 00:33:50.890
and pushback that, that often you do when
you have significant change.

00:33:50.890 --> 00:33:54.050
What we weren't able to stay there for was
people realizing wait,

00:33:54.050 --> 00:33:56.200
okay, it's not so bad.

00:33:56.200 --> 00:33:57.990
Like yes we have a new teacher evaluation
system,

00:33:57.990 --> 00:34:01.100
but now I'm getting paid a whole lot more
money because I'm effective in

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:03.596
the classroom, you know the sky didn't
fall, etc.

00:34:03.596 --> 00:34:07.440
So you know, but those things have to be
in place for

00:34:07.440 --> 00:34:10.530
some time before people can come around to
it.

00:34:10.530 --> 00:34:13.410
And I think that's just a virtue of you
know,

00:34:13.410 --> 00:34:18.270
also sort of leadership in knowing that,
that consistency is important too.

00:34:18.270 --> 00:34:21.250
And I think that one of the most fortunate
things about my experience in D.C.

00:34:21.250 --> 00:34:24.990
is that the woman who was deputy was able
to continue on and

00:34:24.990 --> 00:34:26.220
she is now the chancellor in DC.

00:34:26.220 --> 00:34:30.640
So there was a tremendous amount of
consistency within the policies that we

00:34:30.640 --> 00:34:31.420
were pushing.

00:34:31.420 --> 00:34:32.860
But the dynamics are different, and

00:34:32.860 --> 00:34:36.920
I think that she's, she's, she's doing a
great job of that now.

00:34:36.920 --> 00:34:41.600
[SOUND]
Yes?

00:34:41.600 --> 00:34:43.460
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Katie, I'm a first year
MBA.

00:34:44.680 --> 00:34:47.730
So I wanted to ask you about some of the
erasure scandals that have

00:34:47.730 --> 00:34:49.130
been happening nationally.

00:34:49.130 --> 00:34:51.830
Over the last few years as you know as
there's been increased

00:34:51.830 --> 00:34:56.570
accountability linked with high stakes
testing that's also been tied with teacher

00:34:56.570 --> 00:35:01.260
cheating administrative teaching in New
York, DC, Texas and across the country.

00:35:01.260 --> 00:35:05.770
I'm wondering as a leader how you kind of,
where within this cycle you see

00:35:05.770 --> 00:35:08.848
the real cause beyond simply blaming the
individuals involved.

00:35:08.848 --> 00:35:10.195
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:35:10.195 --> 00:35:12.570
So, I'd say this.

00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:17.530
That, I believe very strongly that the
vast majority of educators in

00:35:17.530 --> 00:35:22.080
this country would never, ever compromise
their personal or

00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:24.420
professional integrity, and cheat on a
test.

00:35:24.420 --> 00:35:27.360
Cuz they know that, that's cheating kids,
and they would never do that.

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:30.010
Does that mean that there aren't a small
number of people out

00:35:30.010 --> 00:35:31.440
there who are gonna make the wrong
decision?

00:35:31.440 --> 00:35:33.800
No, you're always gonna have that
circumstance.

00:35:33.800 --> 00:35:37.600
And I think that because of that you need
to be very clear with folks about the fact

00:35:37.600 --> 00:35:39.350
that cheating is unacceptable,

00:35:39.350 --> 00:35:42.980
you have to have very strong test security
measures in place.

00:35:42.980 --> 00:35:44.400
And then when it does happen, and

00:35:44.400 --> 00:35:48.800
find that this happens, then you have to
have consequences for those folks.

00:35:48.800 --> 00:35:51.500
So, that's, that's generally what I feel.

00:35:51.500 --> 00:35:54.840
I think that the, where this debate is
going or

00:35:54.840 --> 00:35:59.250
where the conversation is going around the
cheating stuff is just weird to me.

00:35:59.250 --> 00:36:01.010
Because what you're hearing a lot is,

00:36:01.010 --> 00:36:05.760
well because there's cheating this shows
that we should not have high stakes test.

00:36:05.760 --> 00:36:08.960
Because when you put these high stakes,
you're making teachers cheat.

00:36:08.960 --> 00:36:14.440
That, that is so demeaning to the teachers
across this country that I know.

00:36:14.440 --> 00:36:17.260
You're, you're implying that because
you're putting, you know,

00:36:17.260 --> 00:36:19.630
because there's pressure that they're
gonna do the wrong thing.

00:36:19.630 --> 00:36:21.940
There's pressure every day in a teacher's
life.

00:36:21.940 --> 00:36:24.420
Forget, forget tests for a moment.

00:36:24.420 --> 00:36:27.890
Because, teachers know that the future of
their kids,

00:36:27.890 --> 00:36:29.960
their students rests in their hands.

00:36:29.960 --> 00:36:32.180
So, they feel that pressure every single
day.

00:36:32.180 --> 00:36:36.330
And they're not gonna make crazy decisions
in, you know, because, just because of,

00:36:36.330 --> 00:36:38.060
they, they feel that pressure.

00:36:38.060 --> 00:36:40.270
So I, I feel like that's,

00:36:40.270 --> 00:36:43.560
that's part of the problem that's going on
in the education debates these days,

00:36:43.560 --> 00:36:47.350
is it is becoming sort of these very
polarized extremes, that because

00:36:47.350 --> 00:36:51.730
you're seeing some of this happen that we
should get rid of standardized tests.

00:36:51.730 --> 00:36:52.750
No.

00:36:52.750 --> 00:36:54.910
You should put the right security measures
in place.

00:36:56.100 --> 00:37:01.890
so, that I, I feel that at the end of the
day, here's the thing.

00:37:01.890 --> 00:37:05.790
There are people out there who are sort of
very anti-testing right now, and

00:37:05.790 --> 00:37:08.495
I actually understand exactly where these
people are coming from.

00:37:08.495 --> 00:37:11.920
Because I can tell you as a parent, I have
two kids.

00:37:11.920 --> 00:37:16.390
And last year, when my younger daughter
was in fourth grade,

00:37:16.390 --> 00:37:20.550
she started coming home in late April and
I, I'd say, well, where's your homework?

00:37:20.550 --> 00:37:22.200
And she'd say, I don't have any homework
anymore.

00:37:22.200 --> 00:37:24.270
In fact, we're hardly going to school
anymore.

00:37:24.270 --> 00:37:27.340
We're only going on field trips cuz the
TCAP is over.

00:37:27.340 --> 00:37:29.880
That's the state test where we live.

00:37:31.480 --> 00:37:36.310
And, I, I thought, oh my gosh, this kid is
saying this to me, right?

00:37:36.310 --> 00:37:40.370
If I was just a normal parent, who didn't
know anything about education reform,

00:37:40.370 --> 00:37:41.820
I would hate that test.

00:37:41.820 --> 00:37:43.680
I would, and, and you didn't even have to
tell me anything about it,

00:37:43.680 --> 00:37:47.780
I'd be like why are we creating an
environment where we're communicating

00:37:47.780 --> 00:37:53.225
either directly or indirectly to kids that
after the test is over learning stops,

00:37:53.225 --> 00:37:55.090
cause that somehow all important.

00:37:55.090 --> 00:38:00.270
So I get the, the, the feelings of the
sort of anti testing because when you

00:38:00.270 --> 00:38:03.050
have experiences like that, that makes you
not wanna like the test.

00:38:04.140 --> 00:38:08.220
At the, on the other side of the equation
though, we have to have

00:38:08.220 --> 00:38:14.160
standardized consistent ways of measuring
whether or not children are learning.

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:18.140
Whether that their learning, their able to
do you know, what, what they should do for

00:38:18.140 --> 00:38:19.160
that grade level.

00:38:19.160 --> 00:38:21.140
So should we have and, and

00:38:21.140 --> 00:38:24.550
standardized tests though they are not
perfect are one way to do that.

00:38:24.550 --> 00:38:26.120
So do we need them, yes.

00:38:26.120 --> 00:38:28.640
Should they be the end all be all,

00:38:28.640 --> 00:38:32.240
the thing that we all go to school around
every, absolutely not.

00:38:32.240 --> 00:38:34.690
So what we need is a balance in the
equation,

00:38:34.690 --> 00:38:36.880
just around with the cheating staff, etc.

00:38:36.880 --> 00:38:38.880
You need, you need balance, you need
reason,

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:41.730
you need rationale, when you are talking
about the policies.

00:38:41.730 --> 00:38:44.639
Because where people are on the extremes,
it makes absolutely no sense.

00:38:47.010 --> 00:38:48.370
yes.

00:38:48.370 --> 00:38:49.490
&gt;&gt; Hi Michelle, I'm Amber.

00:38:49.490 --> 00:38:51.110
I'm a second year MBA.

00:38:51.110 --> 00:38:56.050
I'm a graduate of D.C. public schools and
a former TFA Corps member Houston 2008.

00:38:56.050 --> 00:39:01.160
I particularly like your idea of public
education as a great equalizer.

00:39:01.160 --> 00:39:01.970
One might argue, though,

00:39:01.970 --> 00:39:06.030
by virtue of us being here at Stanford,
that we are the winners in that equation.

00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:10.550
So, my question to you is, how do you get
people involved in the public

00:39:10.550 --> 00:39:14.620
education debate and, I guess, the
movement, particularly considering most

00:39:14.620 --> 00:39:18.560
people that leave here might not go in the
education sector.

00:39:18.560 --> 00:39:23.520
Or if we end up in banking, finance,
consulting, what, what might have you,

00:39:23.520 --> 00:39:26.360
it's easier to say that's a very difficult
problem to solve,

00:39:26.360 --> 00:39:29.590
I'm gonna put my kids into public, or
private school and forget the issue.

00:39:29.590 --> 00:39:30.950
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:39:30.950 --> 00:39:35.450
So that's a great question, and, and one
that, that vexes me every day.

00:39:36.990 --> 00:39:40.080
Because take, take, for example, business
folks.

00:39:40.080 --> 00:39:41.080
Right?

00:39:41.080 --> 00:39:46.820
The vast majority of business people that
I run into on a daily basis say to me,

00:39:46.820 --> 00:39:49.480
you know, Michelle, God bless you.

00:39:49.480 --> 00:39:53.980
You know, god speed, keep doing the work
that you're doing.

00:39:53.980 --> 00:39:55.150
I mean, it's just amazing.

00:39:55.150 --> 00:39:58.440
And I say, great, do you think you could
sit on my board?

00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:00.135
Or help me in the, oh, no.

00:40:00.135 --> 00:40:01.090
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:40:01.090 --> 00:40:01.590
&gt;&gt; Right?

00:40:03.500 --> 00:40:07.110
so, and, and, and literally, if I had a
dollar for every person who told me this,

00:40:07.110 --> 00:40:09.670
like Students First would be set for a
long time.

00:40:09.670 --> 00:40:13.430
And it's, and it's frustrating, but at the
same time, it's understandable, right?

00:40:13.430 --> 00:40:15.850
If you have a CEO of a Fortune 500 company

00:40:17.110 --> 00:40:22.050
who takes a stand on education and then
the unions or whoever like, come after it,

00:40:22.050 --> 00:40:25.420
why are you supporting Students First, why
are you taking a stand on this issue?

00:40:25.420 --> 00:40:28.260
Let's boycott the company, their stock
price is going.

00:40:28.260 --> 00:40:32.480
Then they're not actually fulfilling their
fiduciary responsibilities to

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:34.230
their organization.

00:40:34.230 --> 00:40:39.300
So, while they may think that education is
important, it's not worth it for

00:40:39.300 --> 00:40:42.810
them to take it on, cuz they have other
stuff to worry about day to day, right?

00:40:42.810 --> 00:40:45.150
So that, I think, makes a whole lot of
sense to me,

00:40:45.150 --> 00:40:46.418
and you can't begrudge people for that.

00:40:46.418 --> 00:40:50.970
But that said, I think that part of the,

00:40:50.970 --> 00:40:55.900
the, the dynamic that we need to create is
getting people, not just business people,

00:40:55.900 --> 00:40:58.400
parents who have their whether they have
their kids in public school or

00:40:58.400 --> 00:41:02.930
not to understand that this is an issue
that is going to

00:41:02.930 --> 00:41:06.210
impact all of us in the long run, right.

00:41:06.210 --> 00:41:09.150
Because how you get people involved in a
movement,

00:41:09.150 --> 00:41:14.480
typically, is if it impacts their day to
day life, right.

00:41:14.480 --> 00:41:16.800
I mean, people are busy every day.

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:20.770
Come out to a meeting, come to the
Capitol, write a letter.

00:41:20.770 --> 00:41:23.250
Ugh, I have 52 million things to do, I, I
can't fit that in.

00:41:23.250 --> 00:41:26.540
Unless you say, your kid is not going to
be able to go to this school.

00:41:26.540 --> 00:41:29.520
Then I will clear my calendar to make
things happen.

00:41:29.520 --> 00:41:33.380
And part of what we haven't been able to
do for vast majority of Americans is show

00:41:33.380 --> 00:41:37.180
them how, whether getting involved in
public education and

00:41:37.180 --> 00:41:41.810
this fight or not is gonna impact their
livelihood, their day-to-day life, right?

00:41:41.810 --> 00:41:43.760
It seems too far removed right now,

00:41:43.760 --> 00:41:46.815
and that's what we've gotta kind of
connect to dots on.

00:41:46.815 --> 00:41:48.540
a, just a quick thing.

00:41:48.540 --> 00:41:50.595
This is, so for employers and business
people.

00:41:50.595 --> 00:41:55.400
50% of of employers in a recent survey
said they could not

00:41:55.400 --> 00:42:00.630
find people in their applicant, in their
candidate pool, who had the skills and

00:42:00.630 --> 00:42:03.630
knowledge that they needed to fill
mission-critical jobs.

00:42:03.630 --> 00:42:07.420
In this economy, with this kind of an
unemployment rate, you have

00:42:07.420 --> 00:42:10.960
people who have jobs, and they're saying
they can't find people to fill them.

00:42:10.960 --> 00:42:12.330
That is crazy.

00:42:12.330 --> 00:42:16.070
That means we are not, we don't have an
education system that

00:42:16.070 --> 00:42:20.650
produces kids with the skills to fill the
jobs that are available.

00:42:20.650 --> 00:42:25.520
And this disconnect is only gonna widen as
time goes on.

00:42:25.520 --> 00:42:29.230
So if we are creating a world in this
country where you know, we

00:42:29.230 --> 00:42:33.030
might have the Facebooks and the Googles
and whatever, but all the coders and

00:42:33.030 --> 00:42:37.510
the engineers, half of them are being
employed by people in in India and

00:42:37.510 --> 00:42:39.710
China because that's, that's who has the
skills.

00:42:39.710 --> 00:42:43.050
That is a huge problem for our country at
large.

00:42:43.050 --> 00:42:45.600
And so connecting those dots is one of the
things that I think we

00:42:45.600 --> 00:42:47.490
have to do in order to crack this open and

00:42:47.490 --> 00:42:50.350
really get a broader swath of people
involved in this effort.

00:42:50.350 --> 00:42:55.140
&gt;&gt; [COUGH]
Oh.

00:42:55.140 --> 00:42:58.580
Oh, I'll go back first and then you after
that.

00:42:58.580 --> 00:43:00.070
&gt;&gt; Sorry.
Hi I'm my name is Alan Eindhoven and

00:43:00.070 --> 00:43:03.330
I'm a retired professor of public
management in this school.

00:43:03.330 --> 00:43:04.910
And my question is to ask for

00:43:04.910 --> 00:43:08.220
some clarification and your thoughts about
the unions.

00:43:08.220 --> 00:43:12.650
I read a lot about you know excessive
unfunded pension and

00:43:12.650 --> 00:43:17.680
liabilities, about inability to you know,
fire sexual predators and

00:43:17.680 --> 00:43:19.890
all kinds of awful stuff like that.

00:43:19.890 --> 00:43:23.170
On one hand, and then I have an argument
with my daughter who's been in

00:43:23.170 --> 00:43:26.500
the Department of Public Instruction in
North Carolina.

00:43:26.500 --> 00:43:28.732
And I say the unions is a big problem and

00:43:28.732 --> 00:43:32.210
she says well we don't have teacher's
unions in North Carolina.

00:43:32.210 --> 00:43:34.340
Yet we still has those problems.

00:43:34.340 --> 00:43:38.510
So, help us understand your thoughts on,
on that.

00:43:38.510 --> 00:43:42.110
&gt;&gt; Yeah well, I run into people every day
who say, the unions are the problems,

00:43:42.110 --> 00:43:44.510
if we just, if we didn't have the unions,
everything would be better.

00:43:44.510 --> 00:43:46.050
And the union is the root of all evil.

00:43:46.050 --> 00:43:49.930
I actually don't agree with that at all I
believe that

00:43:49.930 --> 00:43:52.670
unions are doing exactly what they are
supposed to be doing.

00:43:53.700 --> 00:43:59.100
Right, the job of the union, the purpose
of the union existing is to

00:43:59.100 --> 00:44:03.540
look out for the rights, privileges, and
pay of their members.

00:44:03.540 --> 00:44:05.425
And they are doing a bang-up job of that.

00:44:05.425 --> 00:44:06.360
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:44:06.360 --> 00:44:09.290
&gt;&gt; So you can't begrudge them the fact
that they're doing exactly what they

00:44:09.290 --> 00:44:10.500
were created to do.

00:44:10.500 --> 00:44:12.760
You can't hate on that.

00:44:12.760 --> 00:44:16.110
I don't actually think that unions doing
their job is the problem.

00:44:16.110 --> 00:44:18.720
I think the problem is that we have,

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:23.000
to date, not had an organized national
interest group

00:44:23.000 --> 00:44:27.280
with the same heft as the teacher's union,
that's advocating on behalf of kids.

00:44:27.280 --> 00:44:30.320
Because if you had that, then you'd have
balance in the equation.

00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:31.190
Right?

00:44:31.190 --> 00:44:31.820
And, and, again,

00:44:31.820 --> 00:44:34.390
you'd have a powerful force that was out
there saying to politicians,

00:44:34.390 --> 00:44:37.540
if you're gonna vote for this, then, you
know, we'll support you, and if you don't,

00:44:37.540 --> 00:44:43.750
but in the absence of that counterforce,
you end up with a skewed landscape and

00:44:43.750 --> 00:44:46.280
a skewed environment towards the special
interest groups.

00:44:46.280 --> 00:44:47.853
And it's not just the teachers union,

00:44:47.853 --> 00:44:51.011
it's testing companies, it's textbook
manufacturers, you name it.

00:44:51.011 --> 00:44:53.414
There are a lot of adult interests out
there in education,

00:44:53.414 --> 00:44:55.679
who are just doing what they're supposed
to be doing.

00:44:55.679 --> 00:44:59.359
So, the, the, the environment becomes
skewed towards those special interests.

00:44:59.359 --> 00:45:01.855
When you don't have a, a balance in the
equation.

00:45:01.855 --> 00:45:06.950
So that is what I would say is the, is the
problem that we need to be solving for.

00:45:06.950 --> 00:45:08.850
Not how are we going to change the unions.

00:45:08.850 --> 00:45:11.280
So like I said, they're doing what they're
supposed to be doing.

00:45:11.280 --> 00:45:16.870
I will say this that, on some issues, I
don't think the union is,

00:45:16.870 --> 00:45:19.530
is serving their members particularly
well.

00:45:19.530 --> 00:45:22.050
And I'll take the pension issue as an
example.

00:45:22.050 --> 00:45:24.315
If we don't do something to solve the
pension problems,

00:45:24.315 --> 00:45:27.940
the systems are going to go bankrupt and
people who are relying on this in

00:45:27.940 --> 00:45:30.530
the long run are going to be screwed and
that is not good.

00:45:31.960 --> 00:45:36.280
If you actually look at polling of new
teachers.

00:45:36.280 --> 00:45:39.880
Right, so say your gonna create a new
pension system.

00:45:39.880 --> 00:45:44.710
And you grandfather everybody who has more
than five years of experience in, right?

00:45:44.710 --> 00:45:47.110
You can keep your current benefits as they
are.

00:45:47.110 --> 00:45:49.520
And you're just looking at people who have
not yet invested in the program.

00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:54.550
The vast majority of those people
actually, they don't,

00:45:54.550 --> 00:45:55.950
they're not interested, they, they would,

00:45:55.950 --> 00:45:59.540
they wouldn't, they don't prefer a defined
benefits program.

00:45:59.540 --> 00:46:04.580
They would prefer to have a portable, more
flexible pension system.

00:46:04.580 --> 00:46:08.470
so, if moving towards a more flexible
system,

00:46:08.470 --> 00:46:12.290
both solves the problem, and is what more
teachers prefer,

00:46:12.290 --> 00:46:16.130
new teachers prefer, then why wouldn't we
move towards that right?

00:46:16.130 --> 00:46:20.110
So the, that, that's the, the kind of
circumstances where I think that,

00:46:20.110 --> 00:46:23.290
that unions need to sort of be moving
along with us, because it's gonna better

00:46:23.290 --> 00:46:28.710
serve their members, and better serve sort
of the general school district operations.

00:46:28.710 --> 00:46:30.180
Because like I said, right now these pen-,

00:46:30.180 --> 00:46:34.680
unfunded pension liabilities, a huge
percentage of the dollars that

00:46:34.680 --> 00:46:38.460
are being used to try to fill these holes
are being taken out of the classroom.

00:46:38.460 --> 00:46:40.410
So that doesn't benefit their current
members.

00:46:44.680 --> 00:46:46.240
the, yes.
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Chen.

00:46:46.240 --> 00:46:50.570
So my expertise is in, isn't in education,
so please correct me if I'm wrong.

00:46:50.570 --> 00:46:53.500
But my understanding is that
socio-economic level and

00:46:53.500 --> 00:46:56.880
heritage educational level are probably
the biggest determinants of

00:46:56.880 --> 00:46:59.790
learning outcomes by a big margin, by a
really wide margin.

00:46:59.790 --> 00:47:02.460
So I was wondering, based on your
experiences,

00:47:02.460 --> 00:47:07.330
I wonder how much of our problems with the
education today are due to broken policy,

00:47:07.330 --> 00:47:12.150
and how much of it is because of, deeper,
more intractable social problems.

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:13.390
&gt;&gt; Yep.
&gt;&gt; And I think, because of,

00:47:13.390 --> 00:47:18.200
kinda DC's unique situation in terms of
socioeconomic inequality, and

00:47:18.200 --> 00:47:21.000
also learning outcomes that you're, kind
of, uniquely positioned to speak to those.

00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:21.750
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:47:21.750 --> 00:47:23.210
So here's what I, I will say.

00:47:24.500 --> 00:47:28.770
When kids are living in abject poverty,
does it make it harder for

00:47:28.770 --> 00:47:31.400
them to come to school ready to learn
every day?

00:47:31.400 --> 00:47:32.010
Absolutely.

00:47:32.010 --> 00:47:36.130
Does it make it harder to teach them
effectively?

00:47:36.130 --> 00:47:37.600
Yes, unequivocally.

00:47:38.700 --> 00:47:40.750
Those are two realities.

00:47:40.750 --> 00:47:49.120
But, can we use poverty as an excuse for
why kids aren't performing at?

00:47:49.120 --> 00:47:50.250
No.

00:47:50.250 --> 00:47:54.650
I, I staunchly refuse to belive that
because if you look at the research,

00:47:54.650 --> 00:47:56.450
it is very clear.

00:47:56.450 --> 00:48:01.590
That of, of all of the in-school factors
that exist, the teacher quality

00:48:01.590 --> 00:48:05.700
is the factor that has the most impact on
student achievement levels.

00:48:05.700 --> 00:48:09.090
So if you look at a recent study that was
done by Raj Chetty from Harvard.

00:48:09.090 --> 00:48:14.860
It showed that if a kid has just one
highly effective teacher

00:48:14.860 --> 00:48:17.910
in their lifetime, in their 12-year
schooling experience.

00:48:17.910 --> 00:48:22.060
That it increases their lifetime earnings,

00:48:22.060 --> 00:48:25.780
their likelihood of graduating from high
school and going on to college,

00:48:25.780 --> 00:48:28.510
decreases the likelihood that they'll have
a teenage pregnancy.

00:48:28.510 --> 00:48:31.790
Just one, out of the 12 teachers that they
have, just one.

00:48:31.790 --> 00:48:33.560
Increases those things.

00:48:33.560 --> 00:48:37.160
We know, based on a research, that if you
have three highly effective teachers in

00:48:37.160 --> 00:48:40.290
a row, it can literally change a kid's
life trajectory.

00:48:40.290 --> 00:48:42.950
Four in a row and you could actually close
the achievement gap.

00:48:44.080 --> 00:48:50.170
So that's not saying that poverty doesn't
provide a a huge obstacle and

00:48:50.170 --> 00:48:52.670
barrier and challenge to our children
everyday.

00:48:52.670 --> 00:48:55.560
But what it does say is that there are
things that we can be doing

00:48:55.560 --> 00:48:59.950
within the schools everyday that can make
a significant difference in

00:48:59.950 --> 00:49:01.260
these kids' lives.

00:49:01.260 --> 00:49:03.020
And when I say these things often enough
people say well

00:49:03.020 --> 00:49:04.160
you're blaming the teachers.

00:49:04.160 --> 00:49:05.120
You're putting too much pressure.

00:49:05.120 --> 00:49:07.690
I am not blaming the teachers for
anything.

00:49:07.690 --> 00:49:10.580
These problems don't exist because
teachers are doing what they are.

00:49:10.580 --> 00:49:14.360
What I'm saying is, that if we look at the
situation that we're in now, and

00:49:14.360 --> 00:49:16.660
we look at the pot, potential solutions,

00:49:16.660 --> 00:49:20.800
I believe that teachers are the solution
to the problems that we face.

00:49:20.800 --> 00:49:23.650
If we know that three or four highly
effective teachers in the world can change

00:49:23.650 --> 00:49:26.300
a kids life trajectory, why shouldn't we?

00:49:26.300 --> 00:49:29.520
Be aspiring, as a nation, to make sure
that

00:49:29.520 --> 00:49:33.638
every single kid has a highly effective
teacher in front of them every single day.

00:49:33.638 --> 00:49:39.640
That's not anti-teacher, that's just,
that's just wanting to put every kid in a,

00:49:39.640 --> 00:49:43.170
in an environment where they are most
likely to be successful.

00:49:43.170 --> 00:49:45.730
And I, I, I think that, that being
controversial or

00:49:45.730 --> 00:49:47.610
whatever makes absolutely no sense to me.

00:49:47.610 --> 00:49:50.280
We have to know within every sector that
we're in but

00:49:50.280 --> 00:49:53.410
in particular in education, that what we
do matters.

00:49:53.410 --> 00:49:56.240
And I would say this, if you look any,

00:49:56.240 --> 00:50:00.729
at any place in history, over any time
period, any country or whatever.

00:50:01.840 --> 00:50:06.640
The most effective strategy that we know

00:50:06.640 --> 00:50:10.870
to break the cycle of inter-generational
poverty is not a social program.

00:50:10.870 --> 00:50:13.360
It's making sure that kids are getting the
education they deserve.

00:50:17.150 --> 00:50:17.750
&gt;&gt; Hi, Michelle.

00:50:17.750 --> 00:50:19.280
Thank you so much for joining us.

00:50:19.280 --> 00:50:21.030
So there's a two-part question.

00:50:21.030 --> 00:50:23.250
The first, the first part is and

00:50:23.250 --> 00:50:28.200
what lessons can the US learn from some of
the other education systems in the world?

00:50:28.200 --> 00:50:31.530
And the second question is on behalf of
the youth in the top team.

00:50:31.530 --> 00:50:35.720
And we like to close with the question
that we all get asked as we apply to

00:50:35.720 --> 00:50:38.700
the business school, which is what matters
most to you and why?

00:50:41.600 --> 00:50:42.605
&gt;&gt; That's a little heavy.

00:50:42.605 --> 00:50:46.040
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So what lessons can we learn?

00:50:46.040 --> 00:50:50.030
I just finished reading a book that, it
hasn't come out yet, but

00:50:50.030 --> 00:50:51.890
it's gonna be out shortly,.

00:50:51.890 --> 00:50:55.180
That's called The Smartest Kids in the
World and How They Got That Way.

00:50:56.380 --> 00:50:59.580
And it's a book written by a,

00:50:59.580 --> 00:51:05.280
an American journalist who has been sort
of in the, she wrote a big piece

00:51:05.280 --> 00:51:08.990
about me when I was the chancellor, and
she got sort of inundated with all this,

00:51:08.990 --> 00:51:11.930
you know, well, why are you saying that
Michelle Rhee's doing x, y, or z?

00:51:11.930 --> 00:51:14.490
So she started hearing you know from
people who are saying,

00:51:14.490 --> 00:51:17.280
well we need to do what Finland is doing,
we need to do what South Korea is doing.

00:51:17.280 --> 00:51:19.830
And so she decided to go to Finland, South
Korea, and

00:51:19.830 --> 00:51:22.680
Poland to figure out what those systems
are doing.

00:51:24.020 --> 00:51:27.360
And I, the book is fascinating because it
actually tells us a lot about what we

00:51:27.360 --> 00:51:28.330
should and shouldn't be doing.

00:51:28.330 --> 00:51:31.970
We should not be trying to replicate a
situation like South Korea.

00:51:31.970 --> 00:51:32.670
Those people are crazy.

00:51:32.670 --> 00:51:33.905
Those are my people, so I can say this.

00:51:33.905 --> 00:51:35.130
[LAUGH] We're a little crazy.

00:51:36.220 --> 00:51:38.250
Koreans have no moderation.

00:51:38.250 --> 00:51:45.685
We're like, we're nuts, and, you know,
look, everything is focused on like nuts.

00:51:45.685 --> 00:51:46.980
Anyway, so we should not do that.

00:51:49.350 --> 00:51:54.310
But we also can't, can't continue to sort
of believe, you know what?

00:51:54.310 --> 00:51:56.930
If kids are poor, they face too many
challenges.

00:51:56.930 --> 00:51:59.650
There's nothing that we can do to overcome
the pa, you know,

00:51:59.650 --> 00:52:00.730
the challenges of poverty.

00:52:00.730 --> 00:52:02.640
And therefore we're gonna throw our hands
up.

00:52:02.640 --> 00:52:03.950
That's not the right answer either.

00:52:03.950 --> 00:52:04.690
Right?

00:52:04.690 --> 00:52:09.870
And so what I think she sort of called out
of the various countries that are doing

00:52:09.870 --> 00:52:14.440
quite well is that there is number one, a,
a very,

00:52:14.440 --> 00:52:17.300
very strong focus on teacher quality.

00:52:17.300 --> 00:52:21.180
And a lot of these countries do this on
the front end by not allowing any but

00:52:21.180 --> 00:52:24.980
the, buddy, but the highest achieving
people to actually become teachers and

00:52:24.980 --> 00:52:27.180
go into schools of education, right?

00:52:27.180 --> 00:52:30.180
And then holding them to a, to a high
standard throughout.

00:52:30.180 --> 00:52:31.630
So that's one piece.

00:52:31.630 --> 00:52:36.670
A, a ver, a, a curriculum that is very,
very sort of

00:52:36.670 --> 00:52:41.180
focused on having high standards for all
kids is another piece to it.

00:52:42.240 --> 00:52:45.360
And then having accountability on all
levels.

00:52:45.360 --> 00:52:48.910
So not just of teachers and of
administrators, but of students too.

00:52:48.910 --> 00:52:49.780
To say look,

00:52:49.780 --> 00:52:52.290
we're gonna hold you accountable if you're
not doing the right things.

00:52:52.290 --> 00:52:53.500
So I'd say those are some of the,

00:52:53.500 --> 00:52:57.210
the, the lessons that we've learned from,
from other countries.

00:52:57.210 --> 00:52:59.830
And again, if we, if we do things in
moderation,

00:52:59.830 --> 00:53:05.060
I think we can actually vastly improve
where we are as a nation.

00:53:05.060 --> 00:53:06.430
Your second question was what?

00:53:06.430 --> 00:53:09.200
&gt;&gt; What matters most to you, and why?

00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:13.230
&gt;&gt; What matters most to you and why.

00:53:13.230 --> 00:53:18.960
I would say that what matters most to me
is kids.

00:53:18.960 --> 00:53:24.130
I mean this is what, this is what makes me
get up in the morning.

00:53:24.130 --> 00:53:28.350
It makes me want to fight every day for
what is in their best interests,

00:53:28.350 --> 00:53:33.290
because so I, now that I run this
political advocacy organization,

00:53:33.290 --> 00:53:36.870
I have a lot more interaction with our
elected officials.

00:53:38.010 --> 00:53:41.615
Which is unfortunate in a lot of ways.

00:53:41.615 --> 00:53:43.560
[LAUGH] I was not too long ago talking to
one of them,

00:53:43.560 --> 00:53:45.660
who said you know Michelle I get it.

00:53:45.660 --> 00:53:51.000
You, you, your policies make, you know,
logical sense to me.

00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:52.660
And I'm not against them at all, he's like
but

00:53:52.660 --> 00:53:56.850
what you need to understand is, if I vote
for this bill in the way that you want.

00:53:56.850 --> 00:53:58.100
Me too.
You have to understand that I'm

00:53:58.100 --> 00:54:01.440
gonna go back home and then the, the
teachers or

00:54:01.440 --> 00:54:05.760
whoever are gonna be picketing in front of
my office and those are my constituents.

00:54:05.760 --> 00:54:08.410
And I have an obligation to serve them.

00:54:09.660 --> 00:54:11.000
And I said, you absolutely do.

00:54:11.000 --> 00:54:13.320
I said, but when you're talking about your
constituents,

00:54:13.320 --> 00:54:15.490
you need to think about all of your
constituents.

00:54:16.560 --> 00:54:21.458
And at the end of the day you also have an
obligation to serve the children of

00:54:21.458 --> 00:54:22.670
your district.

00:54:22.670 --> 00:54:23.630
And guess what?

00:54:23.630 --> 00:54:26.920
They don't vote, they don't contribute to
campaigns.

00:54:26.920 --> 00:54:30.138
And they don't organize rallies on your
office.

00:54:30.138 --> 00:54:35.249
So if you're just moving and turning
towards where the voices are loudest.

00:54:37.190 --> 00:54:39.975
Necessarily that means your gonna be
turning your backs to kids

00:54:39.975 --> 00:54:40.890
,your back on kids.

00:54:40.890 --> 00:54:42.364
.

00:54:42.364 --> 00:54:43.070
And that is unacceptable.

00:54:43.070 --> 00:54:46.920
You have to be able to do this and this is
what I I believe I believe so

00:54:46.920 --> 00:54:51.890
strongly in what children are capable of
doing.

00:54:51.890 --> 00:54:57.000
I did it when I, throughout my entire term
as a chancellor in DC, I never made

00:54:57.000 --> 00:55:01.940
a substantial decision without talking to
kids and engaging them in the policies.

00:55:01.940 --> 00:55:05.070
Because despite what all the adults were
always saying and

00:55:05.070 --> 00:55:09.330
the arguments that we were having, the
kids were the ones who had the most

00:55:09.330 --> 00:55:14.760
insightful, rational, reasonable
conversations and debates.

00:55:14.760 --> 00:55:16.780
I kid you not, because they're always like
yeah, but,

00:55:16.780 --> 00:55:18.920
and then they're like, you know, go back
and

00:55:18.920 --> 00:55:22.170
forth but show them, like, you're right,
we're all right, but this is what.

00:55:22.170 --> 00:55:26.220
So they could actually do this in a way
that, that adults couldn't.

00:55:26.220 --> 00:55:28.580
The problem is we don't listen to them
enough in my opinion.

00:55:29.794 --> 00:55:35.828
And in the other thing that I believe very
strongly

00:55:35.828 --> 00:55:41.338
is there is nothing that is more worth
fighting for

00:55:41.338 --> 00:55:49.620
than the future of a young person so kids
without a doubt most importantly.

00:55:49.620 --> 00:55:50.315
Thank you.

00:55:50.315 --> 00:55:52.410
[NOISE]

